  <h1>India’s Migration Trends — Internal Mobility and Policy Gaps</h1>
  <div><em>The Hindu | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>Migration has always been central to India’s socio-economic transformation. In 2024, The Hindu highlighted rising internal migration, particularly from poorer states (Bihar, UP, Odisha) to urban and industrial hubs (Delhi, Mumbai, Bengaluru). Post-COVID, migration patterns recovered strongly, but policy frameworks for migrant welfare remain weak. For UPSC, migration cuts across economy, governance, and social justice.
________________________________________
Scale of Migration
•	Census 2011 (latest available): 45 crore internal migrants (~37% of population).
•	Economic Survey 2017: 8–9 million people migrate annually for work.
•	Gendered Migration: Majority women migrate for marriage; growing numbers for work/education.
•	Post-COVID Trends: Migrant workers returned to cities as construction and services rebounded in 2022–24.
________________________________________
Drivers of Migration
1.	Economic Opportunities
o	Rural distress and lack of jobs push youth to urban centers.
o	Pull factors: construction, manufacturing, services.
2.	Education & Health
o	Families migrate for better schools and hospitals.
3.	Climate Change
o	Droughts and floods forcing “distress migration.”
4.	Social Mobility
o	Marriage and kinship continue as cultural drivers.
________________________________________
Challenges Faced by Migrants
•	Informal Work: Most migrants employed in low-wage, insecure jobs.
•	Lack of Social Security: Limited access to ration, healthcare, and housing in destination states.
•	Exclusion from Schemes: Despite One Nation, One Ration Card (ONORC), many still excluded due to Aadhaar/portability issues.
•	Housing Crisis: Migrants forced into slums or rental exploitation.
•	Gendered Challenges: Women migrants face exploitation and unsafe working conditions.
________________________________________
Government Interventions
•	ONORC (2019): Enabled portability of PDS benefits.
•	E-Shram Portal (2021): Registration of unorganized workers, ~30 crore enrolled by 2024.
•	Affordable Rental Housing Complexes (ARHCs): For migrant workers.
•	Labour Codes (2020): Aim to provide portable social security, though implementation uneven.
________________________________________
Challenges in Policy
•	Implementation Gaps: Awareness and access remain weak.
•	Data Deficit: Lack of real-time migration data for planning.
•	Coordination Issues: Inter-state disputes over resource burden.
•	Exclusionary Urbanization: Migrants often treated as outsiders in cities.
________________________________________
UPSC Relevance
•	GS-I (Society): Migration trends, urbanization.
•	GS-II (Governance): Welfare delivery, portability of schemes.
•	GS-III (Economy): Labour, informal sector, employment.
•	Essay:
o	“Migration is not a problem but a driver of development.”
o	“India’s cities are built on the labour of invisible migrants.”
•	PSIR Paper II: Citizenship, rights of marginalized groups, governance of mobility.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Strengthen ONORC: Universal portability with awareness campaigns.<br/>2.	Migrant Data Systems: Regular surveys for better planning.<br/>3.	Housing Solutions: Expand ARHCs with PPP models.<br/>4.	Skill Development: Train migrants for formal, better-paying jobs.<br/>5.	Inter-State Coordination: Create migration councils for cooperation.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>Migration is a silent force shaping India’s economy and cities, but migrants remain socially and politically invisible. Empowering them through portability of rights, housing, and social security will ensure both justice and productivity. For UPSC aspirants, migration exemplifies how economy, governance, and rights intersect in India’s development story.</p>
